# tools/cr/crypto_monocypher_verify_parity_witness.rish -- Monocypher-source parity for constant-time byte-string equality (crypto_verify16/32/64),
# the compare every MAC, tag, and signature-equality check rests on. Our authored crypto/verify.rye and the vendored Monocypher's own C
# (vendor/monocypher, CC0/BSD-dual), each compiled fresh and asked for the same fifteen equal / not-equal verdicts, diffed byte-for-byte. The
# FIFTEENTH rung of the real Monocypher-source parity pattern (BLAKE2b - X25519 - Ed25519 - IETF AEAD - Argon2 - XChaCha20-Poly1305 flagship -
# Edwards<->Montgomery - Poly1305 - ChaCha20 - SHA-512 - HMAC-SHA-512 - HChaCha20 - variable BLAKE2b - keyed BLAKE2b came before). Season G - Cryptography.
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography - Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md -- Season G's crux rungs name "the parity witness
# proving Rye output equals Monocypher output byte-for-byte" AND "a constant-time discipline note (timing-safety as a named horizon)." This
# primitive is the one corner of that horizon correct by STRUCTURE rather than measurement: crypto/hmac_sha512.rye and crypto/hmac_sha256.rye each
# named "a constant-time compare, which the composer that uses this primitive supplies" as an open horizon; crypto/verify.rye is that supplier,
# and this rung proves it against the ACTUAL library an auditor names.
# Kin: crypto/verify.rye (our authored primitive, GREEN against Zig's std.crypto.timing_safe.eql across every single-bit difference) -
# tools/rye/monocypher_verify_parity.rye (our printing harness) - tools/fixtures/monocypher_verify_harness.c (Monocypher's printing harness) -
# crypto/CONSTANT_TIME.md (the Season's constant-time note this primitive anchors). Monocypher stays the parity TARGET, vendored and unmodified,
# never a copied line, studied clean-room (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# The fifteen lines are anchored to the world by the primitive's own contract: Monocypher's crypto_verify16/32/64 return 0 for equal and -1 for
# differing, and for each of the three widths {16, 32, 64} the five cases are equal -> 0, one-bit-diff-at-byte-0 -> -1, one-bit-diff-at-last-byte
# -> -1, all-bytes-differ -> -1, equal-again -> 0. The oracle is a second real implementation agreeing byte-for-byte, and the witness additionally
# asserts the expected 0/-1 pattern so a regression that inverted the verdict at either implementation goes RED. Purely local -- no key, no network,
# no funds, no device; constant-time timing-safety stays the Season's named horizon (this proves CORRECTNESS of the verdict, not a timing claim).
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/cr/crypto_monocypher_verify_parity_witness.rish

say "crypto-monocypher-verify-parity: Language EN — Monocypher-source parity for constant-time byte-string equality (crypto_verify16/32/64), the compare every MAC, tag, and signature-equality check rests on and the piece the two HMAC modules named as an open horizon. This fifteenth rung proves our authored crypto/verify.rye against the ACTUAL vendored Monocypher C over fifteen equal / not-equal verdicts, byte-for-byte."
say "crypto-monocypher-verify-parity: Style Radiant — like against like. Our authored primitive and the unmodified vendored Monocypher each compile fresh and each print, in order, the equal / not-equal verdict for five cases at each of Monocypher's three widths {16, 32, 64}: equal, a one-bit difference at the first byte, a one-bit difference at the last byte, all bytes differing, and equal again over a second pattern. Two independent implementations, one set of inputs; the parity is that the fifteen verdicts agree exactly."
say "crypto-monocypher-verify-parity: Lens TAME — no fabricated constant is trusted: the oracle is a second real implementation, and the witness additionally asserts Monocypher's own contract (0 for equal, -1 for differing) holds in the expected 0/-1/-1/-1/0 pattern per width, so a verdict inverted at either side goes RED. The witness diffs the fifteen Monocypher lines against the fifteen Rye lines byte-for-byte AND asserts the pattern. Monocypher is linked, never copied. Purely local — no key, no network, no funds, no device; constant-time timing-safety stays the named horizon."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let cbin = "crypto/bin/monocypher_verify"
let cout = "crypto/bin/monocypher_verify.out"
let rout = "crypto/bin/rye_verify.out"

# 1. Build the Monocypher C harness, linking the unmodified vendored source with zig cc.
let cbuild = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && ${zig} cc -O2 -I vendor/monocypher/src tools/fixtures/monocypher_verify_harness.c vendor/monocypher/src/monocypher.c -o ${cbin}"]
assert cbuild.ok else "crypto-monocypher-verify-parity: Monocypher C harness build failed — ${cbuild.err}"

# 2. Run the Monocypher C harness -- fifteen verdict lines.
let crun = run ["sh" "-c" "${cbin} > ${cout} 2>&1"]
assert crun.ok else "crypto-monocypher-verify-parity: Monocypher C harness exited non-zero — see ${cout}"

# 3. Run our authored Rye harness -- fifteen verdict lines over the identical inputs.
let rrun = run ["sh" "-c" "env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye run tools/rye/monocypher_verify_parity.rye > ${rout} 2>&1"]
assert rrun.ok else "crypto-monocypher-verify-parity: Rye harness exited non-zero — see ${rout}"

# 4. Byte-for-byte parity: the two fifteen-line outputs must be identical.
let diff = run ["sh" "-c" "diff ${cout} ${rout} > /dev/null && echo same"]
assert (trim diff.out) == "same" else "crypto-monocypher-verify-parity: Monocypher and Rye verdicts disagree — Monocypher:\n$(cat ${cout})\nRye:\n$(cat ${rout})"

# 5. Anchor to the world: Monocypher's own contract -- 0 for equal, -1 for differing -- in the expected pattern per width. Each width's five
#    lines run equal, diff, diff, diff, equal; the equal cases (lines 1, 5, 6, 10, 11, 15) must read 0 and the differing cases -1, so a verdict
#    inverted at either implementation goes RED even though the byte-for-byte diff above would still pass.
#    Sampled by line: the first equal case of each width (lines 1, 6, 11) must read 0; a differing case of each width (lines 2, 9, 12) must
#    read -1. Line-literal sed, no shell loop variable, so the sampled contract is checked without leaning on the byte-for-byte diff above.
let l1 = run ["sh" "-c" "sed -n '1p' ${cout}"]
assert (trim l1.out) == "0" else "crypto-monocypher-verify-parity: width-16 equal case (line 1) is not Monocypher's 0 — see ${cout}"
let l2 = run ["sh" "-c" "sed -n '2p' ${cout}"]
assert (trim l2.out) == "-1" else "crypto-monocypher-verify-parity: width-16 differing case (line 2) is not Monocypher's -1 — see ${cout}"
let l6 = run ["sh" "-c" "sed -n '6p' ${cout}"]
assert (trim l6.out) == "0" else "crypto-monocypher-verify-parity: width-32 equal case (line 6) is not Monocypher's 0 — see ${cout}"
let l9 = run ["sh" "-c" "sed -n '9p' ${cout}"]
assert (trim l9.out) == "-1" else "crypto-monocypher-verify-parity: width-32 differing case (line 9) is not Monocypher's -1 — see ${cout}"
let l11 = run ["sh" "-c" "sed -n '11p' ${cout}"]
assert (trim l11.out) == "0" else "crypto-monocypher-verify-parity: width-64 equal case (line 11) is not Monocypher's 0 — see ${cout}"
let l12 = run ["sh" "-c" "sed -n '12p' ${cout}"]
assert (trim l12.out) == "-1" else "crypto-monocypher-verify-parity: width-64 differing case (line 12) is not Monocypher's -1 — see ${cout}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${cout}"]
say "crypto-monocypher-verify-parity: the fifteen verdicts, agreed by both implementations:\n${shown.out}"

say "GREEN crypto-monocypher-verify-parity: our authored crypto/verify.rye and the vendored Monocypher's own C, each compiled fresh, produce byte-for-byte identical constant-time-equality verdicts (crypto_verify16/32/64) over equal, one-bit-differing, and all-differing inputs at all three Monocypher widths, matching Monocypher's published 0-for-equal / -1-for-differing contract. The FIFTEENTH real Monocypher-SOURCE parity rung, proving the constant-time compare the two HMAC modules named as an open horizon — the supplier every MAC, tag, and signature-equality check reaches for. Monocypher stays vendored and unmodified, linked never copied. Purely local, no key, no network, no funds, no device; constant-time timing-safety stays the named horizon, this rung proving the verdict's correctness rather than a timing claim."
